course: dictionary
lesson: 1009
title: A sweet word from French
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A bonbon is a small sweet, often with a soft or chocolate centre. The word comes from French, where «bon» means good, and bonbon says it twice. The context helps too: «a box of bonbons» is a box full of little sweets.
explain: In Australia and New Zealand the word means something different. There, a «bonbon» is the paper cracker pulled at Christmas dinner. Read the words around it to tell which meaning is meant: people eating or sharing a treat means the sweet, and people pulling it with a bang at Christmas means the cracker.
explain: Because the word is French, the sentences around it can also teach grammar. Compare «J'ai des bonbons» (I have some sweets) with «Je n'ai pas de bonbons» (I don't have any sweets). After pas, des becomes de.
explain: The French question «Combien ?» means "how many?" or "how much?" In «Tu as combien de bonbons ?» the answer must be a number. Ask yourself what kind of answer the question wants, and the words around it will show you.
fact: A bonbon is a small sweet, often with a soft or chocolate centre.
fact: In Australia and New Zealand, a bonbon is a Christmas cracker.
fact: «Bon» means good in French, and bonbon says it twice.
fact: After pas, des becomes de: «J'ai des bonbons» but «Je n'ai pas de bonbons».
fact: «Combien ?» means "how many?" or "how much?"; «Tu as combien de bonbons ?» asks for a number.
exercise: «A box of bonbons was on the table.» What is a bonbon in this sentence? | *A small sweet | A type of cloud | A French town | A large box
exercise: In Australia, Lily pulls a bonbon at Christmas dinner. What is a bonbon here? | *A paper cracker | A carol | A bell | A sweet with a soft centre
exercise: What does «Je n'ai pas de bonbons» mean? | I have some sweets | I have many boxes | *I don't have any sweets | I have one sweet
test: In «Tu as combien de bonbons ?», what kind of answer does the question want? | A name | A yes or no | A colour | *A number
test: The word bonbon comes from French, where «bon» means what? | Big | Loud | *Good | Sweet
exam: Which French word means "how many?" in «Tu as combien de bonbons ?» | Pas | Des | *Combien | Bonbon
